<%- include('../partials/header', { title: 'The Chronicle' }) %>
<%
  const german = A.german;
  const flag = name => [...german].some(g => String(name).includes(g)) ? '🇩🇪' : '🇺🇸';
  const dollars = n => (n == null ? '' : '$' + Number(n).toLocaleString('en-US'));
  const champByYear = {};
  for (const c of A.champions) champByYear[c.year] = c;
  const years = [];
  // The timeline tops out at the CURRENT season, passed in from the route — not
  // a hardcoded 2026, which would silently omit next season the moment it opens
  // and nobody would notice until a chapter went missing. Falls back to the
  // newest harvested season if the live year wasn't supplied.
  const _top = (typeof topYear !== 'undefined' && topYear)
    ? topYear
    : Math.max(A.lore.foundingYear || 2016, ...Object.keys(A.byYear).map(Number));
  for (let y = _top; y >= (A.lore.foundingYear || 2016); y--) years.push(y);
  const linkFor = y => A.byYear[y] ? `/history/season/${y}` : (y >= 2016 && y <= 2022 ? '/history/early' : null);
%>

<div class="chron">
  <!-- 1. HEADER -->
  <p class="chron-eyebrow">Est. 2016 · Not Quite the Same Ten Men · A Decade of Receipts</p>
  <h1 class="chron-title">The <span class="gold">MFGA</span> Archive</h1>
  <p class="chron-sub">Every dollar, every champion, every humiliation — written down at last.</p>
  <% if (typeof topYear !== 'undefined' && topYear) { %>
    <p style="text-align:center; margin:.2rem 0 0"><a class="btn navy small" href="/history/vault/<%= topYear %>">🗄️ The Vault — this season's trash talk &amp; dispatches →</a></p>
  <% } %>

  <!-- 2. THE STORY OF THE LEAGUE (the centerpiece) -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="body">
      <div class="chapter">
        <p>It started with a dick joke. 2016, ten guys, a hundred bucks each, and a
        league called Balls and Wieners — picked by adults with jobs and children.
        That became the Whiny Little Bitch League, which fit better, and in 2024
        somebody decided we should be Make Football Great Again. Three names, none of
        them sayable in front of your mother.</p>

        <p>The buy-in went up every year like a bad mortgage — a hundred, one
        twenty-five, one fifty, on up to four hundred, with a vote on the floor to
        make it five. David has objected to every increase since the year he won it.</p>

        <p>Then in 2023 we added a hundred a week to the top score. Nobody thought
        much about it. It's now thirty-seven percent of the pot — the biggest prize
        in the league, and none of us voted for it to be that.</p>

        <p>Three guys aren't here anymore. Sam, Dylan and Jeremy took the chairs
        Brandon, Taylor Hagen and Tori left behind. The records barely remember them
        — two are scrubbed out entirely, one survives as a name in a single draft
        order. They're in here anyway.</p>

        <h3><span class="n">§</span> The Dark Ages (2016–2022)</h3>

        <p>There are no box scores from the first seven years, which is the single
        greatest act of mercy this league has ever performed for most of you. What
        survives is a list of champions and the checks that went with them, and even
        that is enough to start fights. The whole prehistoric era lives at
        <a href="/history/early">the early years</a>, such as it is.</p>

        <p>Year one, <strong>David</strong> won it for <span class="stat">$300</span>
        back when the buy-in was a friendly little <span class="stat">$100</span> and
        nobody had started lying to each other yet. This matters because David has
        spent every season since standing on the floor objecting to the buy-in going
        up, which is a bold position for a man who has cashed exactly one title in ten
        years and clings to it like a birth certificate. Then <strong>Cory</strong> —
        yes, the same guy who built this website, the guy narrating this to you right
        now, keep that in your back pocket — went back-to-back in
        <span class="stat">2017</span> for <span class="stat">$375</span> and
        <span class="stat">2018</span> for <span class="stat">$450</span>. Write those
        years down. Circle them. That's the whole résumé. He has not won since. Six
        years and counting, a man maintaining a shrine to a championship old enough to
        be in second grade.</p>

        <p>And then the ceiling fell in, and his name was <strong>Marian</strong>.
        <span class="stat">2019</span> for <span class="stat">$600</span>.
        <span class="stat">2020</span> for <span class="stat">$750</span>.
        <span class="stat">2021</span> for <span class="stat">$900</span>. Three in a
        row, an actual, undisputed, look-it-up three-peat while the rest of us paid
        the escalating buy-in like it was a subscription to watching Marian collect.
        Nine other guys funding one man's dynasty and calling it a hobby.</p>

        <h3 id="asterisk"><span class="n">§</span> The One With the Asterisk</h3>

        <p>Then came <span class="stat">2022</span>, and this one gets said straight
        and said once. The final week's Bills–Bengals game was cancelled after Damar
        Hamlin went into cardiac arrest on the field. That is the whole of the serious
        part, and it stays serious. What happened next in our little world of pretend
        is that Marian was losing his matchup, the game that would have finished him
        simply vanished, and he and <strong>Sam</strong> agreed to split first and
        second — a shared <span class="stat">$1050</span>. Marian will tell you it's a
        four-peat. Everyone else will tell you it's a coin that never landed. The ring
        has been disputed at every draft since, and it will be disputed at every draft
        until we're dead. The trophy is fake and the argument is forever, which is
        honestly the most this league has ever gotten for its money.</p>

        <h3><span class="n">§</span> 2023 — The Box-Score Era Opens</h3>

        <p>Everything changes here, because in <span class="stat">2023</span> — our
        last year as the Whiny Little Bitch League — we started keeping every number,
        and we bolted a <span class="stat">$100</span>-a-week weekly-high bounty onto
        the pot without understanding what we'd built. The full ledger is at
        <a href="/history/season/2023">the 2023 season</a>, and it is a rich, rich
        document.</p>

        <p><strong>Michael</strong> won it, and he won it clean, which he will never
        let you forget. He went <span class="stat">95</span>–<span class="stat">40</span>
        against the whole field — the best all-play in the league — and poured in
        <span class="stat">1852.22</span> points, the most of anyone. No cancelled
        games, no split, no coin. Just the best team taking the money. First ring of
        what would become a problem.</p>

        <p>Now. <strong>Cory.</strong> Website Cory finished
        <span class="stat">9</span>–<span class="stat">6</span> and cashed
        <span class="stat">$400</span> while running, by a comfortable margin, the
        worst-scoring team of anyone who touched the money — a
        <span class="stat">.422</span> all-play, a <span class="stat">57</span>–<span class="stat">78</span>
        record against the field, meaning if he'd played everyone every week he'd have
        lost more than half his life. He backed into that check fully sideways. The
        team was named <em>Herbert the Pervert</em>. A man who peaked in 2018 naming
        his fraud lineup after a Family Guy creep and then getting <em>paid</em> for
        it — that's the 2023 season in one sentence.</p>

        <p>Meanwhile <strong>Justin</strong> went <span class="stat">71</span>–<span class="stat">64</span>
        against the field, a genuinely competent year, and finished
        <span class="stat">6</span>–<span class="stat">9</span> with
        <span class="stat">$0</span> in his pocket, robbed blind by the schedule while
        the pervert cashed. That's the game. That's the whole game.</p>

        <p><strong>David</strong> — playing that year under the flag of
        <em>El Travesty</em>, which turned out to be less a team name than a scouting
        report — had the loudest season on record. He posted the year's single highest
        score, <span class="stat">171.50</span> in Week 4, and blew Sam off the earth
        by <span class="stat">98.40</span> in one of the great beatdowns. He also
        dropped <span class="stat">140.76</span> in a game and <em>lost</em>, missed a
        weekly-high bounty by <span class="stat">0.52</span> — fifty-two cents of a
        football point standing between him and free money — and, in the crown jewel,
        benched Justin Fields for <span class="stat">40.98</span> in a week his
        starters coughed up a combined <span class="stat">69.12</span>. He sat more
        than half his final total on the bench and called himself a contender.</p>

        <p><strong>Jeremy</strong> had the best single performance of the entire year
        — C.J. Stroud went nuclear for <span class="stat">51.80</span> — and finished
        <span class="stat">5</span>–<span class="stat">10</span> with a
        <span class="stat">7</span>-game losing streak wrapped around it, which takes a
        special kind of man to own the year's high point and still lose seven
        straight. <strong>Bates</strong> opened the season with a
        <span class="stat">52.20</span>, the lowest score anybody put up all year,
        setting a tone he'd honor for a decade. And <strong>Dylan</strong> made the
        championship game and lost it — the closest that man has ever come to a ring,
        a fact that will matter more and more the longer this goes.</p>

        <h3><span class="n">§</span> 2024 — Make Football Great Again</h3>

        <p>New name, new rules — <span class="stat">2024</span> is when we rebranded to
        MFGA and added keepers, so now you could hold your mistakes over from year to
        year. The <a href="/history/season/2024">full 2024 story</a> is a monument to a
        single stat line, and we'll get to it, but the champion was
        <strong>Jeremy</strong>, off the 2-seed, running the best all-play in the
        league at <span class="stat">88</span>–<span class="stat">47</span> and the
        best lineup efficiency at <span class="stat">91.8%</span>. Actually good,
        actually deserved, took the ring and <span class="stat">$900</span>. And here
        is the beautiful part: in his Week 15 loss on the way there, Jeremy
        <em>benched Jared Goff for <span class="stat">51.06</span></em> and won the
        title anyway. Left fifty on the pine and got fitted for jewelry regardless.
        Some guys are just lucky, and we'll come back to that too.</p>

        <p><strong>David</strong> — this time as <em>Cream Hunt</em>, and no, don't —
        won the regular season and <span class="stat">$950</span> on an
        <span class="stat">11</span>–<span class="stat">4</span> record built on a
        <span class="stat">.593</span> all-play, the luckiest man in the league that
        year, and then face-planted to <span class="stat">4th</span> in the playoffs
        the second the schedule stopped carrying his water. Eleven-and-four in
        September, tourist in January.</p>

        <p>But 2024 belongs to <strong>Cory</strong>, forever, and not the way he'd
        like. Website Cory scored the <span class="stat">2nd</span>-most points in the
        entire league — <span class="stat">1830.64</span>, a genuinely great total —
        and <em>missed the playoffs.</em> He lost the final seed to Marian on the
        season-points tiebreaker by <span class="stat">8.20</span> points, roughly one
        bad handoff across an entire year, while leaving <span class="stat">297</span>
        points rotting on his bench. Then, because the football gods have a sense of
        humor, he missed the Week 15 weekly-high bounty by <span class="stat">1.06</span>.
        The man's team name that year was <em>Zero Mistakes Were Made.</em> He wrote
        that. About that team. On purpose. It is the single funniest thing in the
        archive and he built the archive.</p>

        <p>The rest of the misery got spread around generously.
        <strong>Marian</strong> posted the season high, <span class="stat">166.18</span>
        in Week 2, and rode Cory's own tiebreaker into the last playoff spot Cory
        earned and lost. <strong>Sam</strong>, sailing under <em>Don of a new age</em>,
        went <span class="stat">3</span>–<span class="stat">12</span> with the lowest
        point total in the league (<span class="stat">1493</span>), the most points
        against (<span class="stat">1857</span>), and the only sub-<span class="stat">70</span>
        game anyone managed all year, a <span class="stat">67.98</span> — Cory beat him
        by <span class="stat">75.08</span> in Week 5, which for Cory qualifies as a
        career highlight. <strong>Michael</strong>, the reigning champ, watched Josh
        Allen detonate for <span class="stat">57.88</span> — the best single game of
        the year — and still went <span class="stat">6</span>–<span class="stat">9</span>
        and missed everything, proof that one great player can't save you and that
        nobody in this league is safe. <strong>Jeremy</strong> missed the Week 1
        bounty by <span class="stat">0.12</span> before it stopped mattering, and
        <strong>Richard</strong> beat him by <span class="stat">1.10</span> in the
        closest game of the year, a margin thinner than a rounding error.</p>

        <h3><span class="n">§</span> 2025 — Michael Again</h3>

        <p>And then <span class="stat">2025</span>, laid out in full at
        <a href="/history/season/2025">the 2025 season</a>, where
        <strong>Michael</strong> stopped being a champion and started being a trend.
        He went <span class="stat">12</span>–<span class="stat">3</span>,
        <span class="stat">90</span>–<span class="stat">45</span> against the field,
        ripped off a <span class="stat">6</span>-game win streak, and cashed
        <span class="stat">$1325</span> — the richest single year in league history —
        for his <span class="stat">2nd</span> ring in three seasons. Two of the last
        three are his. Marian built the old empire; Michael is quietly building the
        new one while everyone else argues about a coin flip from 2022.</p>

        <p>The real story of 2025, though, is <strong>Richard</strong>, and it is a
        tragedy. Running as <em>Doener</em>, Richard scored
        <span class="stat">1711.20</span> points — the <span class="stat">3rd</span>-most
        in the entire league — and finished <span class="stat">4</span>–<span class="stat">11</span>,
        stone dead last. His all-play was <span class="stat">74</span>–<span class="stat">61</span>,
        a genuinely good team by every measure that isn't the standings. In Week 15 he
        dropped <span class="stat">142.72</span>, a top-three score that week, and
        <em>lost.</em> It is the largest robbery on record in a league that keeps
        detailed records of robbery. He scored like a contender and finished like
        roadkill, and he still doesn't have a ring to show for any of it.</p>

        <p><strong>Bates</strong>, on the other hand, named his team
        <em>Carried by Gibbs*</em> — the asterisk his own, a rare moment of
        self-awareness — strapped himself to Jahmyr Gibbs (<span class="stat">49.90</span>
        in Week 12), rode him all the way to runner-up, and <em>still</em> has no ring,
        because that's the Bates experience: getting close on someone else's back and
        coming home empty. <strong>Dylan</strong> posted the worst lineup efficiency in
        the league at <span class="stat">84.3%</span> and left
        <span class="stat">308</span> points on his bench, which is a full extra decent
        player he simply chose not to play, all season, on purpose. And
        <strong>Jeremy</strong> and <strong>David</strong> were once again the two
        luckiest men on the field, the biggest positive luck gaps in the league — the
        universe continuing its long tradition of handing wins to guys who benched a
        quarterback.</p>

        <p>Which brings us, as it must, back to <strong>Cory.</strong> Website Cory,
        custodian of ten years of everyone's shame, went
        <span class="stat">5</span>–<span class="stat">10</span> and won
        <span class="stat">$0</span>. The team was named <em>Scheisse.</em> He is a man
        so fluent in his own failure he had to reach into German for it. Seven years
        since a title, an archive of his own greatest hits, and a lineup named after
        the word for shit. He built a cathedral to remember the whole league by and
        the loudest thing in it is the echo of his own 2018.</p>

        <h3><span class="n">§</span> Where It Stands</h3>

        <p>Ten years of checks add up, and the all-time money board is its own kind of
        scoreboard. <strong>Marian</strong> leads at roughly <span class="stat">$5,083</span>,
        disputed coin and all. <strong>David</strong> sits second at about
        <span class="stat">$4,038</span>, funded largely by regular seasons he couldn't
        finish. <strong>Michael</strong> is third and climbing fast at
        <span class="stat">$3,675</span>, and given the last three years he isn't
        stopping. Then <strong>Cory</strong> at <span class="stat">$3,520</span> —
        fourth in career earnings, first in self-documentation — followed by
        <strong>Bates</strong> (<span class="stat">$2,995</span>),
        <strong>Jeremy</strong> (<span class="stat">$2,346</span>),
        <strong>Dylan</strong> (<span class="stat">$1,694</span>),
        <strong>Sam</strong> (<span class="stat">$1,250</span>), and down at the bottom
        the two men the schedule hates most: <strong>Richard</strong> at
        <span class="stat">$950</span> and <strong>Justin</strong> dead last at
        <span class="stat">$600</span>. Four men have never won a single title — Dylan,
        Bates, Richard, Justin — and three of them scored their way to the doorstep
        only to have it slammed on their fingers.</p>

        <p>Now the buy-in sits at <span class="stat">$400</span> with a vote on the
        floor to push it to <span class="stat">$500</span>, and David is objecting
        again, on schedule, because it's an even year. The weekly-high bounty nobody
        thought about has quietly become <span class="stat">37.5%</span> of the whole
        pot — the biggest prize in the league is a thing none of us voted to make the
        biggest prize in the league, and it's the reason a last-place Richard can
        outscore half the field and go home broke. Whatever we vote in 2026, the full
        record of every increase, every rename, every rule we bolted on drunk lives at
        <a href="/history/amendments">the Amendments</a>. Ten years in, and it still
        started with a dick joke.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 3. THE SEASONS timeline -->
  <div class="card">
    <h2>📖 The Seasons <span class="sub">tap a year</span></h2>
    <div class="body">
      <div class="timeline">
        <% for (const y of years) {
             const c = champByYear[y];
             const href = linkFor(y);
             const written = chapters.has(y);
             const preSleeper = y < 2023;
             const inProgress = y === 2026;
        %>
          <% if (href) { %><a class="tl-row" href="<%= href %>"><% } else { %><div class="tl-row tl-dead"><% } %>
            <span class="tl-year"><%= y %></span>
            <span class="tl-mid">
              <% if (inProgress) { %>
                <span class="tl-champ tl-live">Season in progress</span>
              <% } else if (c) { %>
                <span class="tl-champ">🏆 <%= c.champion %> <span class="flag"><%= flag(c.champion) %></span><% if (c.asterisk) { %><a class="aster" href="#asterisk" onclick="event.stopPropagation()">*</a><% } %></span>
                <% if (c.payout != null) { %><span class="tl-won"><%= dollars(c.payout) %></span><% } %>
              <% } %>
            </span>
            <span class="tl-tag">
              <% if (written) { %><span class="tag">Chapter</span>
              <% } else if (A.byYear[y]) { %><span class="tag pending">Records</span>
              <% } else if (preSleeper) { %><span class="tag ghost">Pre-Sleeper</span>
              <% } %>
            </span>
          <% if (href) { %></a><% } else { %></div><% } %>
        <% } %>
      </div>
      <p class="tl-note">Full box scores survive from <b>2023</b> on. 2016–2022 are the age before Sleeper — champions and money only.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4. FOUR DOORS -->
  <div class="doors">
    <a class="door" href="/history/records">
      <span class="door-icon">📖</span>
      <span class="door-title">The Record Book</span>
      <span class="door-sub">All-time highs, lows, the dynasty tracker, and every franchise page.</span>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href="/history/money">
      <span class="door-icon">💰</span>
      <span class="door-title">The Money Board</span>
      <span class="door-sub">Career earnings, ranked ten deep. The table that settles arguments.</span>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href="/history/badbeats">
      <span class="door-icon">💔</span>
      <span class="door-title">Bad Beats Hall of Fame</span>
      <span class="door-sub">Auto-detected heartbreak — the tragedies the schedule authored.</span>
    </a>
    <a class="door" href="/history/catalogue">
      <span class="door-icon">🃏</span>
      <span class="door-title">The Absurdity Catalogue</span>
      <span class="door-sub">Every box score mined: kickers over QBs, benched studs, near-misses.</span>
    </a>
  </div>

  <!-- The 2022 asterisk is stated once, in the story above (#asterisk). -->

  <p class="provenance">
    Derived from the master sheet (<%= A.provenance.master_sha256.slice(0, 12) %>…)
    and the Sleeper harvest of <%= (A.provenance.harvest_built_at || '').slice(0, 10) %>.
  </p>
</div>

<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
